			<div class="content" id="post-content-2732230">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">corrections&nbsp;to&nbsp;corrections</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">to&nbsp;corrections&nbsp;to&nbsp;corrections&nbsp;to...</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>and some notations</p>
<blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>But’s given the heart to its sole son</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Is &quot;but's&quot; a contraction for &quot;but has&quot;? It ought to be but hast</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think &quot;but hath&quot; might be the more correct one there.<br />
Also, hymns sometimes make extreme contractions like &quot;but hast&quot; to &quot;but 'st&quot; or even &quot;b'st&quot; for the sake of flow or fitting all the words into a verse (especially in translations) so that's something to keep in mind for the rest of these.</p>
<blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>You alone are but their lifetime, a candle in the dark</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why the switch to second-person plural pronouns here?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Correction would be &quot;Thou alone art their lifetime, a candle in the dark&quot;<br />
(the &quot;but&quot; doesn't really make grammatical sense there to begin with, so I left it out)</p>
<blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Thou cycles through the sky, sending visions from the heavens</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Thou cyclest</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd suggest a total rework into &quot;In thine cycles through the sky, thou sendest visions from the heavens&quot;</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

